<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>The clause in your policy, in plain terms</h2>
<p>Most homeowners, farm and commercial property forms sold in Nebraska carry a short appraisal condition. The wording varies, but the structure is familiar: if you and the insurer fail to agree on the amount of loss, either side can demand appraisal in writing. Each side picks a competent and impartial appraiser, the two appraisers select an umpire, and a written agreement signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss.</p>
<p>That two-of-three rule is the reason the person you name matters. The award is not advice and it is not an opening offer. Once signed, it fixes the dollar value of the covered loss.</p>
<h2>What we do once we are named</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Check the clock.</strong> We read the demand letter and your policy form to confirm how many days each side has to name an appraiser and whether any other conditions apply.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect and measure independently.</strong> We walk the roof, attic, siding, interior and outbuildings ourselves. We do not start from the carrier’s estimate and mark it up.</li>
<li><strong>Write a line-item valuation.</strong> Our estimate uses current pricing for your part of Nebraska, with code items, matching and overhead and profit handled the way your policy is actually worded.</li>
<li><strong>Work the differences with the other appraiser.</strong> We meet the carrier’s appraiser and go line by line. Most files narrow sharply here.</li>
<li><strong>Agree on an umpire and present what is left.</strong> If items remain open, we help select a qualified umpire and present photos, measurements and pricing support.</li>
<li><strong>Read the award before anyone signs.</strong> We make sure replacement cost, actual cash value and depreciation are separated clearly so the payment that follows is easy to calculate.</li>
</ol>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Keep in mind</b>Appraisal decides how much a covered loss costs. It does not decide whether something is covered, and it is not the place for bad-faith claims. If the carrier denied the claim outright, talk with a Nebraska attorney first.</div>
<h2>Files where representation tends to help</h2>
<p>Appraisal is most useful when coverage is accepted and the disagreement is about scope or price. Common examples in Nebraska: how many roof slopes a hailstorm actually damaged, whether discontinued siding can be repaired without a visible mismatch, what a pole barn re-skin costs after a straight-line wind event, or how much drywall and paint work a frozen pipe really caused.</p>
<h2>How we keep it credible</h2>
<p>We tell you what we expect a panel to accept, not what you want to hear. An inflated estimate can win a client and lose an umpire, and the umpire is the one who signs. Our goal is a number that holds up.</p>

<h2>Questions about this service</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can I use the appraiser my roofer recommends?</summary><div class="a"><p>You can choose anyone who meets your policy’s requirements, but many forms require an impartial or disinterested appraiser. Someone with a stake in the repair contract can give the carrier a reason to challenge the award.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if the carrier ignores my appraisal demand?</summary><div class="a"><p>If the policy has an appraisal clause and the dispute is over the amount of loss, the carrier generally participates once a proper written demand is made. If it refuses, that becomes a legal question for a Nebraska attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long does a typical Nebraska appraisal take?</summary><div class="a"><p>A straightforward residential hail or wind file often wraps up within six to ten weeks of both appraisers being named. Commercial, farm or contents-heavy claims, and files that need an umpire, usually run longer.</p></div></details></div>
